following the dot-com crash as money  dried up and the company's stock crashed  95  the executives set up an emergency  meeting while their e-commerce business  was still growing rapidly it was far  from being profitable so they decided to  launch a platform that helped other  companies to build their own e-commerce  websites called merchant.com hearing the  story you probably thought that this is  how shopify got started right  well this was actually the story of  amazon and their diversification effort  after the dot-com crash but this wasn't  actually all that successful you see  amazon's rapid growth throughout the  1990s had led to a rather unorganized  business environment which made it  extremely difficult for them to  successfully launch merchant.com so  amazon quickly dropped the idea and  started testing out other paths for  diversification six years later though  in 2006 shopify leveraged the same  business model as merchant.com and they  knocked it out of the park so here's the  story of shopify and how they showed up  amazon at their own game  taking a look back the story of shopify  circles back to a man named tobias ludka  who was born in 1981 in copeland's west  germany his father was an internal  medicine doctor while his mother was a  teacher for disabled children from a  very young age toby had a massive  interest in modifying computer hardware  and coding so when he was six years old  his parents bought him a computer  similar to the commodore 64. and within  the next few years toby taught himself  how to write code and develop simple  games despite this innate curiosity to  experiment and learn by himself it  doesn't seem like toby was a big fan of  school whenever a teacher asked him a  question toby preferred to give them  smart witty responses instead of  actually answering the question and toby  took shortcuts whenever possible and  tried to pass his classes by spending  the least amount of effort possible in  my opinion i think toby simply had his  priorities straight from a very young  age but to his parents and teachers this  was quite concerning so they had toby  take a bunch of psychoanalysis exams to  identify if he had any learning  disabilities but they were never able to  diagnose anything and as toby grew older  his behavior from a traditional  perspective simply got worse and worse  toby says that he had a serious  authority problem and hated answering to  teachers so as soon as he could drop out  of school he did at age 16 from 10th  grade toby went ahead and joined a  computer science apprentice program at  simon's but he didn't like this very  much either you see it the  apprenticeship required him to use java  which he felt was a super restrictive  programming language in this world  filled with restrictions and limitations  toby felt that there was only one true  escape which was snowboarding toby would  regularly go on snowboarding trips and  one of these trips took him to whistler  british columbia where he met a girl  named fiona mckeen as you would guess  these two would fall in love and due to  the great distance between germany and  canada they would have to figure out a  living situation initially they agreed  to live in each other's countries in  alternating years first fiona came to  germany and lived with toby for a year  and then toby moved to canada to live  with fiona for a year but he never came  back  despite finding the love of his life  toby was still kind of a bum at the end  of the day he was still a high school  dropout that had no idea what he wanted  to do so he started networking with  people in canada one of the first people  he ran into was a man named scott lake  scott was a family friend of fiona and  would regularly come to family events  and this is when he would meet toby toby  was significantly younger than scott  coming in at 12 years younger but the  two had several similar interests and  soon enough they decided to start a  business together in 2004. the initial  plan wasn't anything crazy or  revolutionary the duo just wanted to  sell something that they were both  passionate about snowboards through the  internet though the idea was simple they  wanted it to be personal in other words  they didn't just want to flip snowboards  on ebay so they decided to launch their  own e-commerce website but all the  e-commerce software available at the  time was absolute crap according to toby  so toby decided to develop his own as  you might expect with toby though he  refused to use any of the popular  programming languages because he thought  that all of them were too restricting so  instead he used an obscure japanese  programming language called ruby ruby  was so obscure that it didn't even have  written documentation in english luckily  for toby though a danish programmer  named david hansen would develop a new  framework for ruby around the same time  which made it much easier for toby to  code this new framework also generated a  lot of interest within the coding space  regarding ruby which got toby involved  in several online forums relating to  ruby toby shared a lot of his software  on these forums such as his software for  processing credit cards and he quickly  became a leading voice within the  community in the meantime toby and scott  were also packaging up snowboards and  sending them to customers but given all  the interest online surrounding toby  software it became pretty clear to toby  and scott that selling software was the  way to go  despite this grand vision neither toby  nor scott had much money so they started  raising money from their friends and  family they were able to raise a total  of two hundred thousand dollars from ten  people with the biggest investors being  fiona's father bruce mckeen and toby's  uncle fun fact this investment would end  up making bruce a billionaire anyway  using these funds arduous went ahead and  hired a german programmer named daniel  waynon who they considered to be a  co-founder of shopify the name shopify  has actually been around since the start  of the company but the company wasn't  actually originally called shopify the  company was actually called a jaded  pixel and shopify was just the name of  the software given the cloud tobii had  amongst online forms they were rapidly  able to build up a base of customers and  start pulling in monthly revenue of  about eight thousand dollars over the  next two years this number would almost  grow tenfold to sixty thousand dollars  per month but this also forced toby to  raise a lot more money which he didn't  really want to do  it's not that toby didn't want to bring  on investors but rather he didn't want  to bring on venture capitalists who he  thought would ruin the company scott  strongly disagreed with his perspective  and felt that venture capital was the  way to go and given the company's  desperate need for cash toby gave in and  started pitching to vcs but no vc really  wanted to invest in an e-commerce  business they felt like they had already  tried that in the 1990s which resulted  in a dot-com crash and a massive losses  so why would they try that again after  failing to raise more capital scott  would leave the company and the toby  would become the ceo of shopify toby  didn't really want to be ceo though he  just wanted to code great software but  with the future of the company at stake  toby would try to teach himself business  he started meeting with a lot more vcs  but these meetings just exposed how  close he was toby wasn't familiar with  the technical business terminology like  margin ratios so he struggled to answer  these questions when vcs asked him but  toby didn't let this stop him after  every vc meeting toby would learn about  the term that he didn't know which  allowed him to approach the next meeting  with just a bit more knowledge slowly  toby's vc meeting started to get better  and better but then the 2008 financial  recession hit which basically made it  impossible for an e-commerce company to  raise money and with that toby knew that  he was truly on his own  the next two years would end up being  the roughest years of shopify's history  toby and fiona had to live with fiona's  parents and her father had to fund  payroll from his savings toby says that  the situation was extremely demoralizing  every day at meetings toby discussed the  long-term goals and vision of shopify  but the truth was that it wasn't clear  if shopify would even survive the next  few months toby refused to focus on the  short term though he was adamant that  they needed to think long term for the  company to truly be successful and since  he couldn't do too much about the  company's financial situation for the  time being he decided to focus on  building a phenomenal team you would  think that toby was looking for genius  programmers that were willing to go all  in on shopify but this couldn't be  further from the truth toby says that he  would hire a team player over a  brilliant jerk any day of the week also  despite all this hardship toby never  pulled an elon he refused to work 100  hour weeks and he refused to force  employees to do that either toby  strongly believes that with creative  work you can't cheat he says that  everyone only has five creative hours  per day and trying to work for longer  hours doesn't lead to better results  while this strongly goes against all the  beliefs of silicon valley toby stuck  with it even during the worst of times  and as the world is slowly recovered  from the financial recession the  interest in e-commerce started to come  back to life and shopify was finally  able to achieve profitability by 2010.  around the same time vcs became more  open to investing in e-commerce once  again and the bessemer venture partners  would agree to invest 7 million dollars  into shopify for 20 of the company in  december of 2010. the following year  shopify would raise another 15 million  dollars and in 2013 they would raise 100  million dollars toby would top all this  off by taking shopify public in 2015  which value the company added just over  1 billion dollars toby had successfully  navigated the company through the  recession and built a team that was  stronger than ever before but shopify  was yet to even receive their biggest  push  up until this point shopify mainly  appealed to small business owners who  wanted to expand their business through  the internet but in 2017 and 2018 we saw  the rise of one of the most infamous get  rich quick schemes of all time drop  shipping if you're not familiar with the  drop shipping it it's basically the idea  to start an online store using shopify  sourcing products on alibaba or  aliexpress and then linking the two  together this way you don't have to  physically deal with a product and your  job is to just market it while it sounds  simple in theory it's actually extremely  difficult now this isn't to say that you  can't make great money through drop  shipping but like with any business the  vast majority fail and it's not nearly  as easy as the dropshipping gurus make  it out to be nonetheless this explosive  trend towards dropshipping was extremely  lucrative for shopify and sent them to  the moon last year shopify reached a  peak market cap of 212 billion dollars  since then shopify stock has actually  gotten destroyed along with the rest of  the market dropping over 60  this has crumbled shopify's market cap  down to just over 80 billion dollars but  this is really nothing to worry about if  we look at shopify's revenue and the  gross profit graphs they haven't even  had a single negative quarter so i find  it hard to believe that shopify will be  fundamentally struggling anytime soon as  for toby his net worth peaked at over 10  billion dollars but it has since cooled  down to just under 6 billion toby  doesn't really care much about these  numbers though while he is super  passionate about shopify and grateful  that he's able to work on what he loves  he also believed that at the end of the  day shopify is just a job and no job  will ever take priority over his family  or personal health just like at the  beginning it looks like toby still has  his priorities straight what do you guys  think about toby's approach to working  do you agree that humans shouldn't work  past a certain point regardless of the  situation comment that down below  also drop a like if you appreciate toby  being the number one donator to team  trees and of course consider checking  out our international channels to watch  the videos in other languages and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm mari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  you

